      "image_path": null,
      "reading_order": 1,
      "needs_vlm": false,
      "extracted_text": "The autopneumatic pump can be used in wells, springs, or lakes where the water is free from sand and mud and does not have to be lifted more than 100 feet, or where the working pressure on the pump does not exceed 65 pounds.",
      "flags": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "r002_text",
      "kind": "text",
      "label": "text",
      "bbox": [
        134,
        527,
        733,
        598
      ],
      "image_path": null,
      "reading_order": 2,
      "needs_vlm": false,
      "extracted_text": "Figure 18 illustrates the application of this system to a farm home.",
      "flags": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "r003_text",
      "kind": "text",
      "label": "text",
      "bbox": [
        131,
        608,
        736,
        828
      ],
      "image_path": null,
      "reading_order": 3,
      "needs_vlm": false,
      "extracted_text": "Pumping.\u2014The water level in shallow wells is usually near enough to the surface to be within the limits of suction. The limiting practical suction lift for a pump is about 20 feet, although it will vary with the elevation above sea level. This means that the pump cylinder which raises the water by suction in lift pumps and which raises by suction and also forces the water in force pumps should not be more than 20 feet above the water level in the well. To practically eliminate suction lift, the cylinder may be submerged as shown in figure 19, thus making the cylinder and pump frame separate and connected only by a section of pipe. This prevents the valves from drying out and makes the pump self-priming. Force pumps often have two cylinders, and in deep wells it is necessary that the lower or suction cylinder be either submerged or within 15 feet of the water level. In the figure shown the forcing cylinder is within the suction cylinder. The suction of any type of pump must be air-tight.",
      "flags": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "r004_text",
      "kind": "text",
      "label": "text",
      "bbox": [
        779,
        695,
        1382,
        772
      ],
      "image_path": null,
      "reading_order": 4,
      "needs_vlm": false,
      "extracted_text": "To find the approximate discharge at each stroke of a pump in gallons use the following table:",
      "flags": {}
    },
